Let $\eta_K = 
u^a \epsilon^b$. In base dimensions: $L = (L^2 T^{-1})^a (L^2 T^{-3})^b = L^{2a + 2b} T^{-a - 3b}$. Equating powers: $2a + 2b = 1$ and $-a - 3b = 0 \implies a = -3b$. Substituting gives $2(-3b) + 2b = -4b = 1 \implies b = -1/4$, which implies $a = 3/4$. Therefore, $\eta_K = \left( rac{
u^3}{\epsilon}ight)^{1/4}$. For the time scale, let $	au_K = 
u^c \epsilon^d$. In base dimensions: $T = (L^2 T^{-1})^c (L^2 T^{-3})^d = L^{2c + 2d} T^{-c - 3d}$. Equating powers: $2c + 2d = 0 \implies c = -d$, and $-c - 3d = d - 3d = -2d = 1 \implies d = -1/2$, so $c = 1/2$. Thus, $	au_K = \left( rac{
u}{\epsilon}ight)^{1/2}$. Permuting the powers leads to incorrect dimensional groupings such as $(
u^2 / \epsilon)^{1/3}$ or inverting the dissipation dependence.
